WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.020
Hi it's Michelle from Lab Muffin
Beauty Science here. Today I'm going to

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.890
be combining my love of skincare science
and sunscreen with my secret love of

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.490
arts and crafts!

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:19.440
Sunscreen. How much sunscreen do we
really need to use? Do you really need to

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.550
use a quarter teaspoon for your face?
I'm sure you've asked yourself this a

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:28.170
few times because a quarter teaspoon is
really quite big and it's such a pain to

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.610
try to slop all that goop on your face.
If it's thick and greasy then it takes

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.829
ages to put on and it's all greasy and
oily, and if it's thin you have to go put

00:00:37.829 --> 00:00:42.149
on so many layers to get it all onto
your face. Where does this quarter

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:46.320
teaspoon rule come from? There are
actually a bunch of different estimates

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.680
for the amount of sunscreen that you
want on your skin. For example the

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.370
Australian Cancer Council recommends
that you use half a teaspoon for your face

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:59.100
head and neck. The NHS from the UK
recommends two teaspoons of sunscreen if

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:04.250
you're just covering your head arms and
neck. The American Academy of Dermatology

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:09.689
recommends one teaspoon of sunscreen for
your face and scalp. Some places even

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:13.979
recommend a full teaspoon for your face
alone. And most of these recommendations

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:17.969
are quite confusing - so they don't tell
you whether or not this includes your

00:01:17.969 --> 00:01:22.859
ears or if it includes various other
bits of your face. These calculations

00:01:22.859 --> 00:01:28.140
come from the fact that sunscreen SPF is
tested at two milligrams of product per

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:32.880
square centimetre, so if you want the
labelled SPF that appears on your

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.950
product's packaging you'll need that
amount. Applying significantly under this

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:42.329
amount will give you uneven coverage
because your skin is quite bumpy and so

00:01:42.329 --> 00:01:45.959
if you don't put on enough then most of
the sunscreen ends up in the valleys and

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:50.880
so the peaks of your skin, the ridges,
won't have enough sunscreen covering

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.420
them. They might even stick out and have
very little sunscreen and get more

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:59.850
damage. So this two milligrams per square
centimeter measurement means that

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:04.439
depending on the size of your face, how
many square centimeters it is, the total

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:08.910
amount of sunscreen that you need is
going to vary. The European Commission's

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.560
Scientific Committee on Consumer Safety
estimates that the 25th percentile of

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:20.160
women have a facial surface area of 565
centimetres squared. This is based on a

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.480
study of women in Denmark and their
total head area, and it

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.040
assumes that half of your head is the same
as your facial surface area. So if we do

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.260
a little bit of maths, that means that we
need two milligrams times 565 square

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:42.150
centimeters, which gives us 1130
milligrams or 1.13 grams. Sunscreen has a

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.860
density of around one gram per mL so
this translates to 1.13

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:51.900
mL. A quarter of a teaspoon is around
1.25 mL so that's around

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.129
right, if we assume that some at
the sunscreen, around 10%, ends up on your

00:02:56.129 --> 00:03:00.870
fingers. Mineral sunscreen - so inorganic
sunscreens - containing large amounts of

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:05.250
zinc oxide, they're going to be a bit
denser, so you can apply around one-fifth

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:09.480
less. Note that when we talk about
teaspoons here we're talking about

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.410
proper measuring teaspoons that are
either 4.93 or 5

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:17.700
mL depending whether you're in the US
or the rest of the world. A study found

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:22.200
that regular teaspoons, the kind that you
eat with, could hold anywhere between

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:26.239
2.5 and 7.5 mL.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:33.110
But obviously different people have
different size faces and so, depending on

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:37.280
how big your face is, or perhaps how
protruding your features are, you're going

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.940
to need a different amount of sunscreen.
Some people have tried to estimate the

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:44.930
size of their own faces in a few
different ways. John Su from Futurederm

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.709
measured the length and width of his
face and multiplied them to give

00:03:48.709 --> 00:03:52.400
620 square centimeters.
Our faces obviously aren't perfect

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:56.480
rectangles, but since our faces are lumpy,
so we've got noses that come out for

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.270
example, the extra area is meant to be
around the same as the area that is

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:08.540
those bits of the rectangle and so as an
estimate that sort of is valid. A Korean

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.650
TV show used a grid that was printed on
plastic to cover a guy's face and they

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.790
worked out that he was 400 square
centimeters. Some people on Reddit have

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:20.169
tried dividing their face up into
different rectangular zones and

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:24.760
approximating their face as an oval,
which gave much lower values of around

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.660
360 square centimeters. So these
estimates have inspired me to try and

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:34.880
find my own way of measuring it. So I
went to this Priceline press event a

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:40.010
while ago and they gave me these rolls
of paper tape. It's their own Priceline

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.669
pharmacy brand, but basically they're a
generic version of 3M's Micropore.

00:04:43.669 --> 00:04:47.840
My parents are nurses so I grew up around
this stuff thinking that this was normal

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.350
sticky tape. This stuff is actually
really amazing for sticking on blisters

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:54.800
on your feet especially near your heel,
and wrapping up paper cuts on your

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.790
fingers, but today what I'm planning to
do with it is waste it all by covering

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:02.419
my face with it. According to Priceline
this paper tape is 2 5 centimeters

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:08.570
by 9.1 meters which altogether gives us
over 2,000 square centimeters. So I

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:12.380
really should have plenty to spare, but I
think I've used some to stick over

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.760
clothing tags which were scratchy - which
is another amazing use of it - so if I run

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:21.229
out I might have to add some band-aids
to the mix. So after I cover my face with

00:05:21.229 --> 00:05:25.160
all of this tape what I'm gonna do is
take it off my face and then stick it on

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:29.870
this grid paper and then count the total
area that it covers. So this way

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:33.140
hopefully I'll be able to measure more
accurately all the lumps and bumps on my

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.100
face with all these bits. Usually I love
maths and

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:41.040
maths is the way I do things but sometimes empirical
research is a bit less of a headache

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.770
unless of course I'm like allergic to
the tape or something. And it's gonna be a

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:49.540
bit of a pain counting all the squares.
My face is pretty oily so I'm gonna have

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:52.510
to wash my face and blot it pretty
thoroughly, and maybe even give it a

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:56.200
quick wipe down with some rubbing
alcohol. But it's possible that if I go

00:05:56.200 --> 00:06:00.130
too slowly then all my face oils will
come out and then unstick the tape and

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:05.590
everything gets ruined. But that's a
problem for Later Michelle. Let's mummify

